<h1>Indexing Conveyors for Precision Positioning</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An indexing conveyor moves product in precise, repeatable start-stop cycles, advancing a fixed distance (the index length) and holding product stationary at a dwell position for filling, labeling, pick-and-place, assembly, or inspection. Buyers also call them positioning conveyors or conveyor positioning systems. Accuracy depends on whether the system locates the belt or a fixtured pallet. <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/2200-series/2200-precision-move-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner rates its 2200 Precision Move servo belt package</a> at ±0.04 in (about ±1 mm) index accuracy, while pallet systems with lift-and-locate stations reach ±0.004 in (about ±0.1 mm) repeatability.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Indexing Works</h2>
<p>Continuous conveyors move product at a constant speed. Indexing conveyors move product in a cycle: <strong>advance</strong>, <strong>decelerate</strong>, <strong>dwell</strong>, <strong>accelerate</strong>, and <strong>repeat</strong>. The dwell period gives downstream equipment, a filler nozzle, a labeler head, a pick-and-place arm, or an inspection camera, time to operate on a stationary target.</p>
<p>The indexing cycle has four critical parameters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Index length (pitch):</strong> The distance the conveyor advances per cycle. This equals the spacing between product stations, from a small container pitch up to a full pallet length.</li>
<li><strong>Index time:</strong> How long the conveyor takes to advance one index length. Shorter index times mean higher throughput. Index time is limited by the acceleration the product can tolerate without tipping or shifting.</li>
<li><strong>Dwell time:</strong> How long product stays stationary at the station. Determined by the downstream operation. A label application is short. A fill cycle is longer and depends on fill volume and product viscosity.</li>
<li><strong>Cycle time:</strong> Index time + dwell time = one complete cycle. Throughput (products per minute) = 60 / cycle time in seconds.</li>
</ul>
<p>The motion profile, how the conveyor accelerates, cruises, and decelerates, directly affects product stability. Aggressive acceleration topples tall bottles. Abrupt deceleration shifts loose product in trays. Servo-driven indexers let engineers tune the motion profile (trapezoidal, S-curve, or custom) to match the product's stability characteristics. Mechanical indexers use fixed cam profiles that balance speed and smoothness for a given application.</p>
<h2 id="tech-heading">Indexing Technologies</h2>
<h3>Servo Indexing Conveyors</h3>
<p>A servo indexing conveyor uses a servo motor to advance the belt a programmed distance, stop it at a programmed position, and repeat, with index length, speed, dwell, and motion profile all set in software. Servo-driven belt indexing is the most versatile and widely used indexing technology for packaging lines. A servo motor drives the conveyor belt through a precision gearbox. The servo controller executes programmable motion profiles, trapezoidal, S-curve, sinusoidal, or custom, with index length, speed, and dwell time all adjustable through the HMI. Dorner's 2200 Series Precision Move, which uses a T10 profile cogged belt and lists precision indexing and timed conveying among its uses, is one example of a servo belt indexer.</p>
<p>Accuracy depends on belt type, drive ratio, and load. Dorner lists ±0.02 in mechanical accuracy and ±0.04 in index accuracy for the 2200 Precision Move with a servo package. Cogged timing belts give the best accuracy because they cannot slip on the pulley. Flat belts are easier for products to slide on and off, but belt stretch and slip reduce positioning accuracy. If the product can shift on the belt, add a fixture, cleat, or stop at the station.</p>
<p>Changeover on servo belt indexers requires no mechanical adjustment. Operators load a new recipe, index length, speed, dwell, and motion profile, from the HMI. This makes servo belt indexing the default choice for lines running multiple SKUs with different package sizes.</p>
<h3>Timing Screw (Starwheel) Indexing</h3>
<p>Timing screws (also called feed screws or worm drives) use a helical screw rotating alongside the conveyor to capture product and space it at a precise pitch. The screw's helix geometry determines the output spacing. Products ride on a continuous flat belt or <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor</a> while the screw controls spacing. This technology is dominant in bottle and container lines where round products need consistent pitch before labeling, filling, or capping.</p>
<p>Servo-driven timing screws adjust pitch by changing the screw speed relative to the belt speed. A timing screw's flight profile is cut for a container shape, so a container that differs much in diameter or profile needs a different screw. Servo drive lets one screw run different pitches and speeds by recipe, but it does not remove the need for screw change parts across container shapes.</p>
<h3>Mechanical Cam &amp; Geneva Drive Indexing</h3>
<p>Mechanical indexing uses physical cams or Geneva drives to convert continuous motor rotation into intermittent motion. A Geneva drive produces a fixed dwell-to-index ratio set by its slot count. A four-slot Geneva indexes during a quarter turn of the driver and dwells for three quarters (3:1). A six-slot Geneva gives 2:1. Geneva motion has abrupt jerk at the start and end of each index, so higher-speed machines often use cam indexers instead. The motion profile is fixed and repeatable without electronic control, making these systems extremely reliable for dedicated, single-format lines.</p>
<p>Cam-driven indexers use barrel cams, globoidal cams, or cylindrical cams to produce custom motion profiles. The cam grind determines the acceleration curve, dwell period, and maximum speed. Changing the motion profile requires machining a new cam, which takes far longer and costs more than a servo recipe change. However, the simplicity of a mechanical indexer (no servo drives, no controllers, no software) appeals to plants with limited controls expertise.</p>
<p>Mechanical indexers excel in high-speed pharmaceutical blister packing, cartoning, and tube filling where the motion profile never changes. <a href="https://www.destaco.com/rotary-positioning/indexers/mechanical-rotary-indexers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DESTACO's CAMCO line</a>, for example, describes its cam-actuated indexers as having a known displacement-time relationship and quick settling in the dwell position, with accuracy listed in arc seconds at the output (12 to 90 arc seconds depending on series).</p>
<h3>Pallet Transfer (Workpiece Carrier) Indexing</h3>
<p>Pallet transfer systems carry product on machined pallets (workpiece carriers) that ride on a conveyor loop. The pallet provides a stable, repeatable reference surface, locating pins, nests, or fixtures on the pallet hold the product in a known position. The conveyor carries the pallet to each workstation, where a lift-and-locate or positioning unit raises it onto locating pins. <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/flexmove/flexmove-pallet-system/lift-locate" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner rates its FlexMove lift-and-locate</a> at ±0.004 in repeatability, and <a href="https://www.boschrexroth.com/en/de/products/industrial-solutions/assembly-technology/conveyor-and-transfer-systems/ts-2plus-transfer-system/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bosch Rexroth</a> states its TS 2plus with TS 2Booster reaches a stop position with an accuracy of up to 0.015 mm.</p>
<p>Workpiece pallet systems such as Bosch Rexroth's TS 2plus, Dorner's FlexMove pallet system, and <a href="https://www.mknorthamerica.com/Products/pallet-conveyor-systems-1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mk North America's VersaMove</a> (which mk describes as a non-synchronous workpiece pallet-based conveyor) are used in medical device, electronics, and other assembly. These systems combine belt or chain conveyor transport with precision locating at each station using pop-up pins, clamps, or servo-positioned stops. The pallet circulates in a loop, loading at station one, indexing through process stations, unloading at the final station, and returning empty to the start.</p>
<p>Pallet transfer indexing suits operations where the same conveyor must handle multiple product variants. Different pallet fixtures accommodate different product shapes. Changeover involves swapping pallets rather than adjusting the conveyor. This approach is common in <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-lift/">packaging line integration</a> for kitting, assembly, and multi-step filling operations.</p>
<h3>Pneumatic Stop Indexing</h3>
<p>The simplest form of indexing uses a continuously running conveyor with pneumatic pop-up stops. The conveyor runs at constant speed. A pneumatic cylinder raises a stop blade into the product path, holding the product at the station. When the downstream operation completes, the stop retracts and the product advances to the next station.</p>
<p>Pneumatic stop accuracy is limited by product momentum, product size variation, and how squarely the product meets the stop. It costs much less than servo indexing and works well for operations that tolerate moderate positional variation. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> with zone stops use this same principle to create multi-zone indexing behavior. On <a href="https://www.shuttleworth.com/products/slip-torque-technology/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shuttleworth's Slip-Torque conveyors</a>, segmented rollers stop under a held product, which keeps back pressure low while upstream product waits.</p>
